Confirmatory result (filing 7e9c6fa2a31b400a3e6238d51e268b3d7e21a35c)
On fresh GSM8K problems in O6's `\nTicket NNNN` template, in BLT-1B with its 512-byte local windows, scored teacher-forced, L8 restores verbatim copies at BLT-1B's default patch rate and at entropy@10%, each compared at a matched rate. At the default rate it gains +73.7 [+70.2, +77.2] points of exact copies, 0.98 of what removing every copy start gains; at 10% it gains +13.8 [+10.8, +17.0]. Its costs stay within the bars, read on point estimates: at most 2 points on computed values, final answers and copied numbers; at most +0.01 bits per byte outside the copy; a patch rate within 0.005; at most 4 new-value removals of about 1,700 computed-value starts at the default rate, and none at 10%.
In BLT-1B with its 512-byte local windows, the fix also holds for 6-digit IDs and mixed IDs in the same `\nTicket` template, at the default rate.
Paired verdict, windowed: L8 lost copies the byte rule kept, in F1 at D: in 5 of the 13 lost copies L8 did not fire at c1 (copies L8 missed); in 8 it fired, and the loss is a side effect of the refit.
Paired verdict, released: Relative to SIV+PO, a rule that stops where the model cannot copy helps under the released conversion; F5's exact copies by text length show where. A byte rule with a length cutoff might match it; that is untested.
No outcome of this test shows that reading the model is needed.
